> This suggests a potential silly patch: when punished and
> hallucinating, make the iron ball and chain act more like a rubber
> ball on an elastic rope (travel further when thrown, reflect off
> obstacles, bounce around at random when dragged, etc.)  Loose iron
> balls should also bounce if thrown while hallucinating.
> 
> Also, all the messages should be changed to refer to the "bouncy
> ball".  Of course, the ball should retain its usual damage-dealing and
> player-dragging abilities.
> 
> (Yes, this would add another obscure situation where deliberate
> hallucination might possibly be useful.  I'd generally consider that a
> good thing.)
Hmm, isn't hallucination supposed to change the way _your senses_ work?
 I don't think it should modify the way _the world around you_ works.
So you might see the ball bouncing, even though it isn't really…

A problem I've always had with hallucination is that experienced players
know quite well what Neiman and Marcus are arguing about.  The messages
stay the same every game.  Randomization of the impressions under
hallucination (you see, you hear, you feel, etc.) either at
initialization or at generation might put some teeth into it, at least
in the early game.

